[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Concurrency, Thread Safety, and Lock-free Problem Solving"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In high-performance systems engineering, concurrent execution is critical for maximizing CPU utilization. However, managing multi-threaded access to shared resources introduces complex failure modes such as data races, deadlocks, and thread starvation. Mastering thread safety requires understanding memory consistency models, atomic operations, and non-blocking synchronization strategies."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Fundamentals of Thread Safety and Data Races"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A data race occurs when two or more threads concurrently access the same memory location, at least one access is a write, and no synchronization mechanisms (such as mutexes or memory barriers) enforce ordering. Thread safety ensures that a data structure or function behaves correctly when invoked by multiple concurrent threads, maintaining internal invariants."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mutual Exclusion: Preventing concurrent execution of critical sections using primitives like mutexes or semaphores."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Immutability: Design objects whose state cannot be modified after construction, rendering them inherently thread-safe."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Thread-Local Storage: Eliminating state sharing by giving each thread its own isolated instance of data."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Modern Hardware and Atomic Operations"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"At the hardware level, processors perform atomic operations—uninterruptible read-modify-write CPU instructions. The foundational primitive for lock-free programming is the Compare-And-Swap (CAS) instruction. CAS checks if a memory location holds an expected value; if it does, it updates the location to a new value in a single atomic transaction."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"// Conceptual representation of atomic Compare-And-Swap (CAS)
bool compare_and_swap(int* reg, int old_val, int new_val) {
    // Executed atomically by hardware
    if (*reg == old_val) {
        *reg = new_val;
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. The Non-Blocking Hierarchy: Lock-Free vs Wait-Free"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Non-blocking data structures avoid traditional mutual exclusion locks to eliminate issues like deadlock and priority inversion. They fall into three main progress guarantees:"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Obstruction-Free: A thread makes progress in a bounded number of steps if all other threads are suspended."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lock-Free: At least one thread in the system is guaranteed to make progress in a finite number of steps. System-wide progress is ensured, though individual threads may suffer livelock."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Wait-Free: Every individual thread makes progress within a bounded number of steps, providing the strongest performance guarantee for real-time systems."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Building a Lock-Free Stack (Treiber Stack)"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Treiber Stack is a classic lock-free algorithm that uses atomic CAS loop operations to manage dynamic head pointers without locking."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"#include <atomic>

template <typename T>
class LockFreeStack {
private:
    struct Node {
        T value;
        Node* next;
        Node(const T& val) : value(val), next(nullptr) {}
    };
    std::atomic<Node*> head;

public:
    LockFreeStack() : head(nullptr) {}

    void push(const T& val) {
        Node* new_node = new Node(val);
        new_node->next = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        // Retry CAS until head is successfully replaced
        while (!head.compare_exchange_weak(new_node->next, new_node,
                                            std::memory_order_release,
                                            std::memory_order_relaxed));
    }

    bool pop(T& result) {
        Node* old_head = head.load(std::memory_order_consume);
        while (old_head && !head.compare_exchange_weak(old_head, old_head->next,
                                                        std::memory_order_release,
                                                        std::memory_order_relaxed));
        if (old_head) {
            result = old_head->value;
            // Note: Memory reclamation requires Hazard Pointers or Epoch-Based Reclamation
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }
};"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"5. The ABA Problem and Memory Reclamation"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"A fundamental vulnerability in CAS-based lock-free structures is the ABA problem. Thread 1 reads value A from location X. Thread 1 is preempted. Thread 2 modifies X from A to B, and then back to A. When Thread 1 resumes, CAS succeeds because location X still reads A, despite the state having changed underneath."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Double-Width CAS (DW-CAS): Pair raw pointers with a monotonically increasing sequence or version counter."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hazard Pointers: Threads declare active references to nodes in global memory, preventing other threads from prematurely freeing nodes during pop operations."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Epoch-Based Reclamation (EBR): Defer memory deallocation until all threads that entered an epoch prior to node removal have exited their critical sections."}]
